# Copy to .env (gitignored) for `docker compose up`.
#
# Configuration now lives in the per-plugin config files (data/config/config.mock.json,
# data/config/config.mosip.json, data/config/config.sunbird.json) plus your
# gitignored data/config/config.local.json
# overlay — not here. This file selects WHICH plugin config to mount and carries
# only the values that differ per run. Anything set here overrides both config
# layers, so you can still pin any field ad hoc.
#
# First run:
#   cp data/config/config.local.example.json data/config/config.local.json   # <- required, it is mounted
#   cp .env.example .env
#   docker compose up --build --abort-on-container-exit --exit-code-from harness
#
# Dry run first — resolves the same config and prints what would run, without
# executing anything and without needing the suite up (--no-deps keeps Compose
# from pulling and starting the suite just to print the plan):
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps harness -c /app/config.json --check
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE UNCOMMENTING ANYTHING BELOW
#
# An empty assignment is NOT the same as "unset". `FOO=` in this file reaches
# the container as FOO="" (verify with `docker compose config`), and the harness
# overrides a config field whenever the variable is PRESENT, empty included
# (config.applyEnv uses os.LookupEnv). So a stray `KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=` here
# silently blanks the secret your mounted data/config/config.local.json supplies, and the
# run fails in a way that looks nothing like a config problem.
#
# That is why every optional override below ships COMMENTED OUT rather than
# empty. To fall back to the config files, leave the line commented — never set
# it to "".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which plugin config to mount as /app/config.json
CONFIG_FILE=data/config/config.mock.json

# Conformance suite image tag. Keep this close to "suite_version" in
# data/conformance/oidcc-test-plan.smoke.json — the smoke allow-list is curated against
# that release. The two may drift when a curated-against tag is pruned (as
# 5.1.41 was): the allow-list still names the release it was validated on, while
# this pin has to name one that still pulls.
#
# The default below can go stale: GitLab prunes old conformance-suite tags, so
# a pin that worked when this file was last updated can 404 by the time you
# clone it. If `docker compose up` fails to pull the server/httpd images,
# check currently published tags and set this to one that still exists:
# https://gitlab.com/openid/conformance-suite/-/releases
SUITE_IMAGE_TAG=release-v5.1.42

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional overrides — uncomment only the ones you want to CHANGE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Target eSignet. Same variable name eSignet itself reads for its public URL,
# so one env block can configure a deployment and the harness aimed at it.
# Either a deployed environment...
# MOSIP_ESIGNET_BASE_URL=https://esignet-thunder1.esdev.mosip.net/v1/esignet
# ...or one you run yourself on this host (`make.sh run` / `go run ./cmd/esignet`
# in ../esignet-service), reached via the container's Docker host alias:
# MOSIP_ESIGNET_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080

# Secrets, if you would rather not keep them in data/config/config.local.json
# KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=paste-the-secret-here
#
# ADMIN_TOKEN replaces the whole KEYCLOAK_* round-trip above for a target that
# does not enforce scope — scope middleware in esignet-service only installs
# when both ISSUER_URL and JWKS_URL are set, so a locally started server never
# inspects this bearer, and any non-empty value works. Explicit opt-in, not a
# fallback: leave it unset for a real deployment, where a broken IAM must still
# fail the run rather than be routed around.
# ADMIN_TOKEN=local-no-scope
# INDIVIDUAL_ID=8267411574
# ID_TYPE selects which identifier kind INDIVIDUAL_ID is (uin|vid|phone|email);
# the conformance and e2e surfaces use it to pick the matching login-id tab, so
# without it a non-phone identifier can be sent through the wrong flow input.
# ID_TYPE=phone

# Narrow the run without editing the config file. Every config.<plugin>.json
# selects all three surfaces, so leaving this unset means conformance runs — and
# that needs a plan config in conformance-suite-private/ (docs/conformance-suite.md)
# or the run stops at NOT RUNNABLE YET. Uncomment the first line for a run that
# needs nothing beyond MOSIP_ESIGNET_BASE_URL and the Keycloak credentials.
# SURFACES=api,e2e
# SURFACES=e2e
# SURFACES=conformance,api,e2e
# TEST_PROFILE=smoke

# How long the harness polls the suite's /api/runner/available before giving up
# and running anyway. Covers the suite's Mongo + Java cold start; compose
# defaults it to 150 (run-all.sh's own default outside compose is 90).
# SUITE_WAIT_SECONDS=150

# TLS verification, per target. These are three separate knobs on purpose:
#
#   CONFORMANCE_TLS_VERIFY  the OpenID suite. Compose defaults it to false for
#                           the bundled suite's self-signed localhost cert —
#                           override it when CONFORMANCE_BASE_URL points at a
#                           remote suite with a real cert.
#   ESIGNET_TLS_VERIFY      the eSignet deployment (conformance login flow, the
#                           mock-SMTP OTP socket, and the e2e surface).
#   API_TLS_VERIFY          the same eSignet, from the godog module.
#
# The last two default to ON and should stay there: those connections carry the
# test identity, its OTP and its password. Set them false only for an eSignet
# with a self-signed cert of its own — never because the suite needed it.
# CONFORMANCE_BASE_URL=https://localhost.emobix.co.uk:8443
# CONFORMANCE_TLS_VERIFY=false
# ESIGNET_TLS_VERIFY=false
# API_TLS_VERIFY=false
